field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}+5{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=4{c}_{n-1}+7n$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $7n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=4{b}_{n-1}+{n}^{2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}+6{c}_{n-2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term ${n}^{2}$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "non-linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "linear"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "homogeneous"] ["text" "inhomogeneous"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253362" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and \nis\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-2}$ appears in the second power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is\n$\\text{non-linear}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "non-linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265142" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "non-linear"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "non-linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "linear"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "homogeneous"] ["text" "inhomogeneous"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253360" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n.$ \n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$  and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=n$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "non-linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "linear"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "homogeneous"] ["text" "inhomogeneous"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-253365" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and\nis\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The term ${a}_{n-1}$ is not in the first power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is $\\text{non-linear}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96257c6d488ad7c3eba54943b695f53bf3033d4da63a73d9ea0d3e69070a3096"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "non-linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "q-265152" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "non-linear"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f1cb9489dd3305aa49961946f721828978e386cfa2a891527ad0cf044ba4ed5"} {"question" "e-16539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2},\n\\qquad\na_1 = 2, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1, \n\\quad\nn \\geq 3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16539.json" "file_sha256" "7dbc156b794ad83f5d228c9df425ead58e7b97e4e2608e296358865d0c179747"} {"question" "e-16539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2,$ and $a_2 = 1,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5{a}_{2}+{a}_{1}=5\\cdot 1+2=5+2=7 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =5{a}_{3}+{a}_{2}=5\\cdot 7+1=35+1=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16539.json" "file_sha256" "7dbc156b794ad83f5d228c9df425ead58e7b97e4e2608e296358865d0c179747"} {"question" "e-16541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n\n- $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n\n- $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16541.json" "file_sha256" "84a09863034a74a7c157634defcbc4d347b4dfdd9fdb1b7d4ab2814bcf2f1454"} {"question" "e-16541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n,$ and $s_i$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}.$\n\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1},$ $c_{n-2},$ and $c_{n-3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16541.json" "file_sha256" "84a09863034a74a7c157634defcbc4d347b4dfdd9fdb1b7d4ab2814bcf2f1454"} {"question" "e-16555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1},\n\\qquad\na_1 =0, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1,\n\\quad\nn \\geq 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16555.json" "file_sha256" "deac2c0094f9f4d52f01b16e15eb3c70ed279dc88989f19aa87ed12e9381c666"} {"question" "e-16555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation $a_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are  constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}.$ So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}},$\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/example-16555.json" "file_sha256" "deac2c0094f9f4d52f01b16e15eb3c70ed279dc88989f19aa87ed12e9381c666"} {"question" "q-253351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "56"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "7584923d522b1817ea27f83c051a789763d34662802e1b66410a0ab51c036545" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "Starting from a1=2 and a2=4, compute a3=3·4+2·2=16, then a4=3·16+2·4=48+8=56. Original option c (zero-based index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. The captured submission was 40 and was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment, not a successful grade for 56. Stored 48 omits the required term 2a2=8." "bindings" {"problem" "a09b05e892ec8baa8bbeea20b89bdf618773492ddeed211b29c0486bb9545d78" "worked_solution" "d0a5b9c724dd521204107e8926d0424911126bb1a978b0f97d080571a44bae62" "original_field" "f1086a8216d0daf1dbd88943f4fbfb0a1b574b01dda18971e0b6f94b6a868669" "reviewed_field" "fb34f3c165af32426a94ea9777e54a1be9f3949239f1a0c602a97dabec8841e3" "raw_widget" "e0d45b1ad2ec4bdd5a937efefcab854e39ef621b2f87f93c37066988f228ccea"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-253351-before.png" "sha256" "6dac5fb6e8b3542b24a1244e247a99b345a7dcacc88f894a38b1b4acbb7be3c1"} {"path" "q-253351-before.json" "sha256" "d460b13cba4dee35ee03bf138c05604ad790097ecef7fca861395f9c2437ba42"} {"path" "q-253351-after.png" "sha256" "d9a254185239a9ce4f8761bb12f207d4325480008e9890add16a2e9e0db210ce"} {"path" "history-q-253351.png" "sha256" "8cf332671b7786faafd6bd1e25786dccc0a24660eec876a5357bccb8eba0ce9c"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "210ac6c019cfbb14f9ec7dd413a1d0ed6a7bcbebdd7fb63a891edec2a7da3cf2"}]} {"question" "q-253351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "56"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036387/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "7584923d522b1817ea27f83c051a789763d34662802e1b66410a0ab51c036545" "rationale" "Starting from a1=2 and a2=4, compute a3=3·4+2·2=16, then a4=3·16+2·4=48+8=56. Original option c (zero-based index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. The captured submission was 40 and was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment, not a successful grade for 56. Stored 48 omits the required term 2a2=8."}] "usage" {"e-16541" [] "q-253143" [] "q-253351" [] "q-253360" [] "e-16555" [] "e-16539" [] "q-252990" []} "basis" 1329 "retractions" [[17592186129508 :question/problem "Find the fifth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3, a_{1} = 5, n \\ge 2$."] [17592186129511 :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 3a_{n - 1} + 2a_{n - 2}, a_{1} = 2, a_{2} = 4, n \\ge 3$."] [17592186129511 :question/answer-fields 17592186126852] [17592186129510 :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n1. $a_{n} = 2a_{n - 2}$\n2. $b_{n} = b_{n - 1}^{2} + 2b_{n - 2}$\n3. $c_{n} = 2c_{n - 1} + n$"] [17592186129513 :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3n^{2}, a_{1} = 10, n \\ge 2$.\nThe recurrence relation $a_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}."] [17592186128819 :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n$a_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}, \\qquad a_1 = 2, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 3$."] [17592186128819 :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2$, and $a_2 = 1$, we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 1 \\\\\na_{3} &= 5a_{2} + a_{1} = 5 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\\\\na_{4} &= 5a_{3} + a_{2} = 5 \\cdot 7 + 1 = 35 + 1 = 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36$."] [17592186128820 :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n2. $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n3. $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$"] [17592186128820 :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero.\n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n$, and $s_i$ is constant for each $i$.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}$.\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1}$, $c_{n-2}$, and $c_{n-3}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186128821 :question/problem "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n$a_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}, \\qquad a_1 =0, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 2$."] [17592186128821 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n$a_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)$\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation\n\n$$\na_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}\n$$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2$. So, the relation has order $2$.\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$. So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$,\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}}$,\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186126835 17592186126836 17592186126837 17592186126838 17592186126839 17592186126847 17592186126848 17592186126849 17592186126850 17592186126851 17592186126853 17592186126854 17592186126855 17592186126856 17592186126857 17592186126869 17592186126870 17592186126872 17592186126873 17592186126874 17592186126876 17592186126877] "immutable_fields" [[17592186126834 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126834 :answer-field/id] [17592186126834 :answer-field/key] [17592186126834 :answer-field/type] [17592186126846 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126846 :answer-field/id] [17592186126846 :answer-field/key] [17592186126846 :answer-field/type] [17592186126852 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126852 :answer-field/id] [17592186126852 :answer-field/key] [17592186126852 :answer-field/type] [17592186126868 :answer-field/id] [17592186126868 :answer-field/key] [17592186126868 :answer-field/type] [17592186126871 :answer-field/id] [17592186126871 :answer-field/key] [17592186126871 :answer-field/type] [17592186126875 :answer-field/id] [17592186126875 :answer-field/key] [17592186126875 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
